  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 AM Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Well below average temperatures in cold air damming \nwill continue for the next couple of days and will be accompanied \nthrough tonight by additional waves of elevated convection and \nlocally heavy rain.\n\n\"Overrunning\" lift/isentropic upglide, atop a surface frontal zone \ndraped from far sern NC wswwd across the cntl Savannah Basin and TN \nValley, will maintain widespread overcast and mixed character rain \n(ie. a combination of light rain/drizzle and embedded showers) \nthrough tonight and also support at least a couple of waves of \ndeeper elevated convection and related locally heavy rain. The first \nwave of elevated convection will focus this morning over srn NC, \nwhere 925 mb WAA/isentropic ascent and elevated instability nearest \nthe frontal zone to the south will be maximized. The second will be \nrelated to the approach and passage tonight of a mid-level shortwave \nperturbation approaching from MO. The latter feature may yield a \ncouple of areas of concentrated, heavier rainfall amounts, including \nagain across srn NC where MUCAPE and deep moisture will remain \nmaximized, but also possibly over the nw Piedmont, where LPMM fields \ndepict a secondary maximum. An extension in time of the Flood Watch \nthat remains in effect for srn NC will be considered, as will the \nissuance of a new Watch for a portion of the nrn Piedmont later \ntoday should model and observational trends continue to suggest a \nsecondary maximum may indeed result there. \n\nIt will otherwise remain unseasonably mild, with high temperatures \naround 10 F below average in the aforementioned CAD overcast and \nperiods of rain; and the record low maximum temperature of 81 F \ntoday at FAY has a very realistic chance of being set. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... By the end of the weekend, expect a return to more \ntypical late July weather, with highs around 90 and mostly diurnally \ndriven convection.\n\nBy Sunday, the front that has been around central North Carolina \nshould have moved far south to have minimal impact locally, and \nhighs should rebound into the 80s everywhere. By Monday, highs will \nreturn to more typical upper 80s and lower 90s, continuing through \nthe week. Precipitation should also return to generally being \ndiurnally-driven. While coverage should be isolated most days, the \ngreatest coverage should occur Tuesday night into Wednesday with a \nboundary moving through the region.",
